5, 0.051849365234375, 0.04815673828125, 0.0270538330078125, 0.0161590576171875, 0.01428985595703125, -0.007904052734375, -0.04248046875, -0.0021305084228515625, -0.00829315185546875, 0.0189208984375, -0.01392364501953125, -0.0069427490234375, 0.022247314453125, -0.0177001953125, -0.03485107421875, 0.00010132789611816406, -0.005664825439453125, 0.0145111083984375, -0.0033740997314453125, -0.0215911865234375, -0.0311737060546875, 0.007137298583984375, -0.00017702579498291016, -0.03302001953125, 0.0249481201171875, -0.0108184814453125, 0.0220947265625, -0.0121917724609375, -0.035400390625, -0.0318603515625, -0.00978851318359375, 0.006282806396484375, -0.027313232421875, 0.0023956298828125, 0.02593994140625, -0.0148468017578125, 0.019683837890625, -0.0170135498046875, -0.003082275390625, 0.036407470703125, -0.004016876220703125, 0.017303466796875, 0.0139617919921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Recent research has begun to investigate the application of Geometric Algebra (GA) to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, particularly in the context of electrical circuits. GA offers a unified mathematical language that can represent geometric transformations and relationships more naturally than traditional vector or matrix approaches, potentially simplifying the analysis of multidimensional systems and providing new insights into their structure.\n\nA central mathematical tool in the study of dynamical systems is the Laplace transform, which converts ordinary differential equations (ODEs) from the time domain to the Laplace (frequency) domain, facilitating their solution and analysis. However, the standard Laplace transform is defined for real or complex-valued functions, and its extension to functions valued in more general algebras\u2014such as GA\u2014remains nontrivial. This extension is necessary to fully leverage the expressive power of GA in the analysis of systems whose states or signals are naturally represented as multivectors.\n\nPrevious works have considered extensions of the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras, such as quaternions and Clifford algebras (see, for example, Hitzer & Sangwine, 2013; Brackx et al., 2001). However, these approaches are often limited in scope or do not address the full generality of geometric algebras relevant to engineering applications. In this work, we introduce a precise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions within the framework of Geometric Algebra. Specifically, for a multivector-valued function \\( f(t) \\) in a geometric algebra \\( \\mathcal{G}(p,q) \\), we define its Laplace transform as\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a scalar or, in some cases, a suitable GA element, and the exponential is interpreted via the GA exponential map. We discuss the main theoretical challenges, including the non-commutativity of multivector multiplication and the interpretation of the exponential function in GA.\n\nOur approach is applicable to geometric algebras with signature \\( p+q \\leq 5 \\), a restriction arising from the computational complexity and the availability of explicit exponential forms in low-dimensional algebras. This limitation ensures that the Laplace transform and its properties can be explicitly computed and applied to practical problems in engineering and physics.\n\nIn summary, the objectives of this work are: (1) to motivate the use of GA in the analysis of dynamical systems and electrical circuits; (2) to provide a rigorous definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in GA; and (3) to clarify the scope and limitations of this extension. Our results lay the groundwork for further applications of GA-based methods in system theory and circuit analysis."}}